  "codebook": {
    "definition": "A statement is classified as 'credit-claiming' if the senator explicitly claims personal or collaborative responsibility for securing a tangible benefit, directing federal funding to their state, achieving a favorable government or administrative decision, or successfully passing legislation or an amendment. The core element is the association between the senator's efforts and the successful delivery of a specific governmental action or resource.",
    "clarification": "Texts should be labeled as credit-claiming if they announce specific federal grants, funding awards, local projects, military base expansions, or disaster relief, as congressional announcements inherently imply the member's role in delivering federal resources to their state. It also includes statements where the senator notes that a specific amendment, provision, or bill they authored, co-authored, or championed has successfully passed a committee or the full chamber, or if they take credit for resolving a casework issue with a federal agency.",
    "positive_examples": [
      {
        "text": "Sen. Mike DeWine today announced that Troy Township Fire Department will receive $78,046 as part of his Firefighter Investment and Response Enhancement (FIRE) Act. Senator DeWine wrote and passed the FIRE Act in 2000.",
        "explanation": "The senator announces the delivery of a specific federal grant to his constituency and explicitly claims credit by tying the grant to legislation he authored and passed."
      },
      {
        "text": "Senator Max Baucus announced today that $12.5 million for vital military projects in Butte has been included in the Defense Appropriations Bill. 'Jon and I were proud to go to bat for these dollars,' Baucus said.",
        "explanation": "The senator explicitly claims personal and collaborative credit for securing specific federal funding allocations in a piece of passed legislation."
      },
      {
        "text": "The Medicare Administrator confirmed that seniors affected by the hurricanes will have until the end of the year to file. The testimony responds to urgent calls for clarification made by U.S. Senator Mary L. Landrieu.",
        "explanation": "The press release links a favorable administrative decision directly to the senator's prior advocacy, claiming credit for the favorable policy outcome."
      }
    ],
    "negative_clarification": "Texts should NOT be classified as credit-claiming if the senator is merely introducing or co-sponsoring a bill that has not yet passed. Expressing an opinion on a policy issue, criticizing the opposing party or the administration, urging an agency to take a future action, announcing a town hall meeting, or presenting a purely ceremonial award does not qualify because these actions lack the successful delivery of a concrete benefit or legislative milestone.",
    "negative_examples": [
      {
        "text": "Today Senator Edward M Kennedy introduced legislation to enhance stem cell research and to strengthen anti cloning provisions. The bill makes human cloning of any kind a felony.",
        "explanation": "The senator is only announcing the introduction of a bill. Because the bill has not passed or delivered a benefit, this is considered position-taking rather than credit-claiming."
      },
      {
        "text": "U.S. Sen. John Cornyn today sent a letter to U.S. Department of Homeland Security Secretary Michael Chertoff, requesting that he give due consideration to a proposal to incorporate the levees.",
        "explanation": "The senator is advocating for a future benefit by urging an agency to act, but has not yet secured the benefit or government action."
      },
      {
        "text": "Senator Ben Nelson voted against both resolutions because he supports setting measurable goals for progress in Iraq and not arbitrary timetables and dates.",
        "explanation": "The senator is explaining his vote on a general policy resolution. This is purely position-taking and does not involve securing a specific benefit or passing legislation."
      }
    ]
  }
}
